ext mutation path outside the lock. Do not fix restoreSourceAfterJointFailure as one more seam. First build the instrument that closes the class: a write-path census of every code path that creates, replaces, removes or renames config.json, its backups, trust.json, the pending joint-commit marker, the lock file and the credential files — apply, rollback, restore-after-joint-failure, Recover/convergeLocked, Initialize, JointCommit leftover handling, Enroll/Rotate/Revoke/MarkRetiring, backup publication, and any helper (writeTempReplace, replaceDurably, secureStaged, CreateExclusive) — as a table with, per row: the production call site, the lock state it requires (exclusive with converged intent / shared fast path / none), the intent revalidation it performs before writing (current generation, marker state, exact preview/source), the positive test, the interleaving negative (a surviving reader or another writer completing or superseding the operation between the failure and the compensation), and the narrowing plant. Enforce the census with a gate that fails when a filesystem mutation of the pair happens outside a held exclusive authorization: either a static gate over the hosttrust/config packages (every caller of the replace/remove helpers must be reachable only from a lock-held context, with a control plant that proves the gate sees a new unlocked caller) or a runtime assertion in the helpers keyed to the lock holder, with a behavioral test for each row. Attach the census as TASK-260909-2ez769_write-path-census-rev6.md.\n4|\n5|Then repair F1 against the census: error compensation after a failed JointCommit (apply and rollback) must run inside the same authorization transaction or reacquire the exclusive lock and revalidate the exact intent (pending marker state, current generation, configuration bytes/digest) before choosing a converged outcome; if another process completed, superseded or converged the operation, the compensation must not restore the old configuration — it refuses or adopts the converged state with the generation change intact. Port TestReviewerRestoreCannotOverwriteConvergedCommit RED first (the seam that turns trust.json to 0644 so custody validation refuses the trust commit, then a surviving LoadCoherent converges to Config4/generation 4, then the failed writer resumes), keep it as a committed test, and add deterministic production tests for apply and rollback trust-commit failures racing (a) a surviving reader, (b) another writer, (c) a held boundary. Keep the CR4 repairs green (surviving-reader convergence, first-open lock preservation, stale-generation refusal, revocation contention) and re-run the original seven reviewer probes. Note the reviewer bound on verifyLockFile's 500ms retry (custody rechecked, inode not pinned): document it as a stated bound or pin the inode, but do not claim protection against an owner replacing lock files externally unless you implement it.\n6|\n7|Retain rows 15 and 21 exactly as CR5 accounted them (Windows native tests present, runtime unverified; matcher-only, consumer handoff persisted); do not inflate them. Update the 21-row AC matrix with actual calls and named tests; rerun the hosttrust and config mutation batteries on the exact final source with per-plant raw logs, a neutral SURVIVED control applied through the same harness, exits from subprocess return codes, and no compile-failure/unapplied/empty-selection kills. Full configured local validation before the normal producer handoff; no hosted CI. Read the Curator-managed go-testing-tools skill first. Leave the candidate UNCOMMITTED for the handoff snapshot. Attach TASK-260909-2ez769_results-rev6.md, TASK-260909-2ez769_write-path-census-rev6.md and TASK-260909-2ez769_producer-evidence-rev6.tar.gz, then hand off through task-board and exit promptly. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse muse-spark max. The independent reviewer will be codex gpt-6-astra low.","final_result":true}}}
